<div class="page-wrap prose">
  <section id="claims" aria-labelledby="claims-title">
    <h2 id="claims-title">Read the sentence before you trust it</h2>
    <p>
      Debezium's documentation says it provides <a href="{{ '/glossary/#at-least-once' | url }}">at-least-once</a>
      delivery. A vendor page says its service delivers &ldquo;with exactly-once semantics&rdquo;. Both
      can be true, because a guarantee belongs to a <em>hop</em>, not to a pipeline. This page is the
      companion to <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/#per-hop' | url }}">Exactly-Once Semantics</a>: that page
      gives the summary table and the idempotent-consumer patterns, and this one goes down to the
      settings, shows what each guarantee covers, and ends with how to check what <em>your</em> pipeline
      gives.
    </p>
    <p>
      Five questions scope any exactly-once sentence:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Which hop?</strong> Database to connector, connector to topic, topic to topic, topic to sink, or the sink's own store.</li>
      <li><strong>Which producer or connector, and is the opt-in switched on?</strong> Many guarantees are off by default.</li>
      <li><strong>Which sink?</strong> Does it share a transaction with the offsets, or only receive a call?</li>
      <li><strong>What happens on failover?</strong> A crash, a restart, a rebalance, a second worker that thinks it owns the task.</li>
      <li><strong>What dedupes, on which key, ordered by what?</strong> The answer that matters at the end is a primary key and a source log position, not a timestamp.</li>
    </ol>
    <table>
      <caption>Phrases you will meet, and the question each one needs</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Phrase</th>
          <th scope="col">What it leaves open</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;delivers events to your destination with exactly-once semantics&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Which hop, and which destination. Is the destination write idempotent, or does the service only retry until it is acknowledged?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;restart from the last successfully processed LSN, guaranteeing that every change is captured exactly once without data loss or duplication&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Captured by what, and processed where? A restart position makes the read side resumable; it says nothing about duplicates that the restart itself can cause, or about the destination.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;Exactly-once processing&rdquo; (stream processors)</th>
          <td>Exactly-once inside which system? For Kafka Streams it is Kafka topics and state stores, not an external database.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;Transactional&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Whose transaction, spanning what? A Kafka transaction does not include your database.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;Idempotent&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Idempotent on which key, and does a replay of an older change overwrite a newer row?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">&ldquo;No duplicates&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Visible to whom? A default Kafka consumer reads records from aborted transactions.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      The first two phrases come from
      <a href="https://streamkap.com/resources-and-guides/sql-server-cdc-setup-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Streamkap's SQL Server CDC guide</a> and
      <a href="https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-change-data-capture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud's change data capture explainer</a>
      (read 2026-10-09). They are examples of wording that leaves the hop and the mechanism open, not
      claims we found to be wrong. Streamkap's sentence names a destination but no mechanism, and its
      page does not mention at-least-once delivery or idempotent writes. Google's sentence is about
      restarting the read from the log and does not mention a destination.
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>As of:</strong> 2026-10-09, against the Debezium 3.7 documentation (the exactly-once page exists
      for Debezium 3.3 to 3.7 and is absent from 3.2) and the Apache Kafka 4.1 documentation. These
      pages change; if a setting below does not match your version, trust your version's documentation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hops" aria-labelledby="hops-title">
    <h2 id="hops-title">Four hops, and what each guarantee means</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>What a CDC pipeline guarantees at each hop, by default and with the opt-ins</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Hop</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
          <th scope="col">What can change it</th>
          <th scope="col">What you still do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">1. Database to connector (reading the log)</th>
          <td>
            At-least-once. Debezium's page says no changes are missed but a record may be delivered
            more than once, and its FAQ explains why: after a crash the connector restarts from the last
            position it managed to record.
          </td>
          <td>Nothing in Debezium. The page states that Debezium has no internal deduplication layer.</td>
          <td>Carry the source log position on every event (<a href="{{ '/event-envelope/' | url }}">event envelope</a>) so a replay can be recognised later.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">2. Connector to Kafka topic</th>
          <td>
            At-least-once. Without the opt-in, Kafka Connect writes the records and then, separately,
            the source offsets, so an interruption between the two repeats the records on restart
            (KIP-618).
          </td>
          <td>
            The opt-in Kafka Connect exactly-once source mode, <a href="#connect-eos">next section</a>.
            Documented by Debezium from 3.3, with caveats.
          </td>
          <td>Read the topic with <code>isolation.level=read_committed</code> if you opt in, and still treat the topic as possibly duplicated.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">3. Topic to sink (the consumer side)</th>
          <td>
            At-least-once. Kafka delivers at-least-once unless the writes and the offsets commit together.
            Kafka Connect has no exactly-once framework for sinks: whether one provides it depends on the
            connector. Debezium's JDBC sink documents at-least-once delivery.
          </td>
          <td>
            Inside Kafka: transactions and Kafka Streams <code>exactly_once_v2</code>
            (<a href="#kafka-to-kafka">below</a>). To an external system: only the sink's cooperation.
          </td>
          <td>Make the write idempotent (<a href="#sink">sink section</a>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">4. Sink to its own store</th>
          <td>Whatever the store's transaction gives you. A single database transaction is atomic.</td>
          <td>Storing the consumed offsets in the same transaction as the rows, where the sink supports transactions.</td>
          <td>Upsert on the primary key, apply only if the incoming source log position is newer, keep deletes as markers.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Nothing in the table adds up to a single transaction from the database to the sink. The guarantee
      across independent systems is not achievable as delivery; the achievable thing is
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#effectively-once' | url }}">effectively-once</a> processing: duplicates
      may arrive, and the sink makes them harmless.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="connect-eos" aria-labelledby="connect-eos-title">
    <h2 id="connect-eos-title">Hop 2 in practice: the Kafka Connect opt-in</h2>
    <p>
      Kafka Connect supports exactly-once for source connectors from version 3.3.0, in distributed mode
      only (<a href="https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-618%3A+Exactly-Once+Support+for+Source+Connectors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KIP-618</a>,
      built on the transactions of <a href="https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-98+-+Exactly+Once+Delivery+and+Transactional+Messaging" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KIP-98</a>).
      Debezium's page documents it for the MariaDB, MongoDB, MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL and SQL Server
      connectors. The settings it lists:
    </p>
<pre><code># Every Connect worker (distributed mode, Connect 3.3.0 or later)
exactly.once.source.support=enabled

# The connector configuration, added to your existing Debezium settings
"exactly.once.support": "required",
"transaction.boundary": "poll"        # the default; Debezium requires poll</code></pre>
    <h3>What the framework does when you switch it on</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Records and offsets commit together.</strong> With the default <code>poll</code> boundary
        Connect starts a Kafka transaction for each batch a task returns from <code>poll</code>, writes the
        batch and its source offsets inside it, and commits. After a crash either both are there or
        neither is, so a restarted task resumes from an offset that matches what is in the topic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zombie tasks are <a href="{{ '/glossary/#fencing' | url }}">fenced</a>.</strong> Each task gets a transactional ID of the form
        <code>&lt;group.id&gt;-&lt;connector&gt;-&lt;task id&gt;</code>. A new producer with that ID bars the
        older one from writing, which stops a task that has been replaced but is still running.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The check happens before you start.</strong> With <code>exactly.once.support=required</code>
        the framework asks the connector whether it can provide exactly-once with that configuration. If it
        cannot, or the worker validating the request does not have exactly-once enabled, the request to
        create or validate the connector fails instead of silently running at-least-once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rolling out is two steps on an existing cluster.</strong> Set
        <code>exactly.once.source.support=preparing</code> on every worker first, then <code>enabled</code>.
        The workers and the connectors also need ACLs for the transactional IDs and the offsets topic, which
        Debezium's page leaves to the Kafka documentation.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The framework can only do this for a connector that provides meaningful source offsets for each
      record and can resume from exactly the position an offset names, which is why the Kafka documentation
      says exactly-once source support &ldquo;is highly dependent on the type of connector you run&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <h3>What it does not do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>It covers the connector-to-topic hop. It does nothing for the sink, and the topic you read may still contain duplicates produced by other paths (an upstream replay, a re-snapshot you triggered, a second connector).</li>
      <li>A consumer on the default <code>isolation.level=read_uncommitted</code> returns records from aborted transactions too. A Kafka Connect sink must set <code>consumer.isolation.level=read_committed</code> to benefit.</li>
      <li>A <code>read_committed</code> consumer returns messages only up to the last stable offset, so it sees a batch only after that batch's transaction has committed.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>The caveats Debezium itself lists</h3>
    <p>
      Debezium's page has a &ldquo;Known issues and considerations&rdquo; section. In short: it says it is
      unclear whether the implementation is fully correct, that no comprehensive study has analysed the
      correctness of transactions in recent Kafka releases, and that two Jepsen reports (on Redpanda and on
      Bufstream) raise concerns about the Kafka protocol and its implementations, particularly for Bufstream.
      It lists three Apache Kafka issues as open and reasons that, since Connect relies on Kafka transactions,
      they may affect Connect too. Their status in Apache JIRA on 2026-10-09:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>The three Kafka issues Debezium's page lists, as JIRA showed them on 2026-10-09</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Issue</th>
          <th scope="col">Title in JIRA</th>
          <th scope="col">Status</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href="https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-17754" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KAFKA-17754</a></th>
          <td>A delayed EndTxn message can cause aborted read, lost writes, atomicity violation</td>
          <td>Resolved (Fixed) on 2026-08-12. A committer's comment there says it &ldquo;should be resolved by KIP-890 and its related followups&rdquo;; no fix version is recorded, so check your broker release.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href="https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-17734" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KAFKA-17734</a></th>
          <td>KafkaConsumer.close(0) can block indefinitely in ConsumerNetworkClient.poll</td>
          <td>Open</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><a href="https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-17582" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KAFKA-17582</a></th>
          <td>Unpredictable consumer position after transaction abort</td>
          <td>Open</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The Debezium page lists all three as open, so the page and JIRA disagree on the first. This page does not
      know whether the two open ones affect a Debezium connector in practice, and neither does Debezium's page:
      it says it is reasonable to assume they might. That is why the opt-in is a reason to test, not a reason to
      drop the idempotent sink. <a href="https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-890%3A+Transactions+Server-Side+Defense" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KIP-890</a>
      describes the delayed-message problem and the server-side defense (a new epoch for each transaction).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kafka-to-kafka" aria-labelledby="kafka-to-kafka-title">
    <h2 id="kafka-to-kafka-title">Inside Kafka: transactions, offsets and read_committed</h2>
    <p>
      If a step reads a topic and writes another topic (a filter, an enrichment, a join), Kafka can make
      that step exactly-once, and Kafka's design documentation is explicit about where it stops.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The idempotent producer is not enough on its own.</strong> <code>enable.idempotence</code>
        defaults to <code>true</code> in current Kafka and removes duplicates from producer retries, but KIP-98
        notes that the guarantee holds within a single producer session: a restarted producer gets a new
        producer ID.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A transactional producer</strong> (a <a href="{{ '/glossary/#kafka-transaction' | url }}">Kafka transaction</a>) sets <code>transactional.id</code>, which also makes a restarted
        instance abort its predecessor's in-flight transaction. It writes the output records and calls
        <code>sendOffsetsToTransaction</code> so the consumer's offsets commit atomically with them
        (<a href="https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-447%3A+Producer+scalability+for+exactly+once+semantics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KIP-447</a>
        lets the producer pass the consumer's group metadata). The consumer needs
        <code>isolation.level=read_committed</code> and <code>enable.auto.commit=false</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Kafka Streams</strong> does this for you: <code>processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2</code>
        (the default is <code>at_least_once</code>; v2 needs brokers 2.5 or newer). The documentation scopes it
        to the input offsets, the state stores and the output topics, committed atomically.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The scope is the sentence to remember: the Kafka documentation says exactly-once delivery
      &ldquo;for other destination systems generally requires cooperation with such systems&rdquo;. A processor that
      also writes to a database, a warehouse load or an HTTP endpoint is outside the transaction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sink" aria-labelledby="sink-title">
    <h2 id="sink-title">Hop 3 and 4: the sink, where it ends</h2>
    <p>
      Kafka Connect's documentation says it can provide exactly-once for sink connectors, but it depends on the
      connector, and the one framework-level instruction is the consumer isolation level. Debezium's JDBC sink
      says it guarantees events are processed at least once, tells you that after a restart or rebalance it can
      receive the same event again, and tells you to set <code>insert.mode=upsert</code> to make the writes
      idempotent. Its <code>delete.enabled</code> setting is off by default, so out of the box a delete event does
      not remove the row. The page describes upsert as an update-or-insert on the primary key; it does not describe
      comparing a source log position, so read your sink's documentation for what happens when an older change
      arrives after a newer one.
    </p>
    <h3>Offsets in the sink's transaction</h3>
    <p>
      Kafka's own documentation describes the pattern for a destination that has transactions
      (<a href="{{ '/glossary/#offsets-in-sink-transaction' | url }}">offsets in the sink transaction</a>): let the consumer
      store its offset in the same place as its output, because a two-phase commit between the offset store and the
      output is usually not available. For a relational database, the <code>KafkaConsumer</code> documentation says
      this lets you commit the result and the offset in a single transaction, and it lists the steps: set
      <code>enable.auto.commit=false</code>, save the position with each write, and on restart or assignment
      <code>seek</code> to it. The Kafka design documentation gives a Connect connector that writes data and offsets to
      HDFS together as its example.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here is that pattern with a position guard added. The statements use PostgreSQL syntax
      (<a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE</code></a>);
      the table and column names are examples. One transaction per Kafka record, or per batch:
    </p>
<pre><code>CREATE TABLE customers (
  id       bigint PRIMARY KEY,
  name     text,
  deleted  boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false,   -- a delete is a marker, not a DELETE
  src_lsn  bigint  NOT NULL                  -- source log position of the last applied change
);
CREATE TABLE sink_offsets (
  topic text, part int, next_offset bigint NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (topic, part)
);

BEGIN;
INSERT INTO customers (id, name, deleted, src_lsn)
VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE
  SET name = EXCLUDED.name, deleted = EXCLUDED.deleted, src_lsn = EXCLUDED.src_lsn
  WHERE customers.src_lsn &lt; EXCLUDED.src_lsn;      -- older or equal position: no-op
INSERT INTO sink_offsets (topic, part, next_offset)
VALUES ($5, $6, $7)                                  -- the record's offset + 1
ON CONFLICT (topic, part) DO UPDATE SET next_offset = EXCLUDED.next_offset;
COMMIT;</code></pre>
    <p>
      On start or partition assignment, read <code>next_offset</code> from <code>sink_offsets</code> and
      <code>seek</code> there. Run on one customer key, the same two statements behave like this (the logic was run
      in SQLite 3.54, which accepts the same <code>ON CONFLICT ... WHERE</code> form; it was not run on PostgreSQL):
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>One key, four Kafka records, and what the two guards each catch</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Kafka offset</th>
          <th scope="col">Change (source position)</th>
          <th scope="col">Row after</th>
          <th scope="col">Which guard acted</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">40</th><td>name = a, position 100</td><td>a, 100</td><td>None needed: first write.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">41</th><td>name = b, position 110</td><td>b, 110</td><td>None needed: newer position.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">57</th><td>name = b, position 110 (the source re-emitted the same change after a crash)</td><td>b, 110 (0 rows changed)</td><td>Position guard. The offset is new, so the offset table cannot recognise it.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">58</th><td>name = a, position 100, arriving late</td><td>b, 110 (0 rows changed)</td><td>Position guard.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The two guards cover different failures. Storing the offset with the row means a consumer that crashes
      between the write and the offset commit cannot leave them out of step: the transaction rolled back or it
      did not. It does not help with a duplicate that is already in the topic at a different offset, because that
      is a different Kafka record. The position guard is what turns that into a no-op, and it also covers
      backfills and replays that rewind the consumer. That is why the idempotent, position-ordered upsert stays
      required even where the first hop is exactly-once. For deletes, keep the row with <code>deleted = true</code> so an
      older change cannot bring it back; the <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">merge cookbook</a> and
      <a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}">materialization</a> pages show the same guard in warehouse
      <code>MERGE</code> form.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="decide" aria-labelledby="decide-title">
    <h2 id="decide-title">What guarantee do I really have?</h2>
    <p>Answer these in order; each one says what you can claim.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Is there a step that reads a topic and writes a topic, using a transactional producer or Kafka Streams
        with <code>exactly_once_v2</code>?</strong> Then that step is exactly-once with respect to its input and output
        topics, as long as every consumer of the output reads <code>read_committed</code>. Claim that, and nothing past
        the topic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Is the destination an external system (database, warehouse, search index, HTTP call)?</strong>
        You do not have exactly-once delivery. Go to 3.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Can the destination hold your consumer offsets in the same transaction as the data?</strong> If yes, a
        consumer crash cannot double-apply or skip a record. If no, a crash between the write and the offset commit
        repeats a record, and only the guard in step 5 protects you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Is the connector-to-topic opt-in off, or on?</strong> Off: the topic can contain duplicates after a
        connector crash. On: fewer, with the caveats above; still assume some.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Is the write an upsert on the primary key, applied only if the incoming source log position is
        newer, with deletes kept as markers?</strong> If yes, you have <em>exactly-once processing</em> for this sink:
        any duplicate or replay leaves the same table. If no, you have whatever the sink's default is, and an older
        change can overwrite a newer one.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Step 5 is the one that holds regardless of the answers to 3 and 4, which is why the rest of this site treats
      at-least-once as the default and the idempotent sink as the guarantee.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="verify" aria-labelledby="verify-title">
    <h2 id="verify-title">Verify what your pipeline gives</h2>
    <p>
      A configuration that says &ldquo;required&rdquo; does not prove behaviour. Three checks, in the order of
      cost. None of these ran against a live Debezium and Kafka stack for this page; the commands are written from
      the Kafka tool documentation, and the <code>jq</code> pipeline was run on a three-line sample only.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Look for duplicates in the topic.</strong> Duplicates from the source show up as the same key and the
        same source position at two different offsets. With the JSON converter and schemas enabled the path is
        <code>.payload</code>; adjust it for your converter.
<pre><code>kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic dbserver1.public.customers --from-beginning --timeout-ms 10000 \
  --isolation-level read_committed \
| jq -r '[.payload.source.lsn, .payload.op, (.payload.after.id // .payload.before.id)] | @tsv' \
| sort | uniq -d</code></pre>
        Every line printed is a change that appears more than once. Run it again with
        <code>--isolation-level read_uncommitted</code> to see what a default consumer sees: with the opt-in on, the
        difference is records from aborted transactions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inject the duplicates yourself.</strong> Stop the sink connector, rewind its consumer group, start it
        and compare the table with a copy taken before. The Kafka documentation describes
        <code>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --reset-offsets --to-earliest --execute</code> (the consumer instances must be
        inactive). If the table changes, the sink is not idempotent. This is the cheapest test of the claim that
        matters.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Kill the connector mid-batch.</strong> Send <code>SIGKILL</code> to the Connect worker while changes are
        flowing, restart it, and run check 1. With the opt-in off you expect duplicates if the kill landed between
        writing records and recording offsets; with it on you expect none visible to a <code>read_committed</code>
        consumer. Fail the run if you injected no duplicates, since then the sink was never exercised. The
        <a href="{{ '/lab-kafka-debezium/' | url }}">Kafka + Debezium lab</a> gives you a stack to try it on, and the
        <a href="{{ '/ops-offsets/' | url }}">offsets and replays</a> page covers rewinds.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="try-it" aria-labelledby="try-it-title">
    <h2 id="try-it-title">See the failure and the fix</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/#demo' | url }}">interactive demo on the Exactly-Once page</a> steps through
      a consumer that writes to the sink, crashes before committing its offset, and re-processes the message, then
      shows the idempotent version. It demonstrates the sink side of hop 3; it does not simulate the Kafka Connect
      opt-in.
    </p>
  </section>

